{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/heterogeneous-line-fill-overreach.md", "content": "# Heterogeneous line-fill demos: do not transfer a surface rule

## Signal from this task family
A stream can return demos with unrelated grid sizes, background/foreground colors, and sparse layouts, while one test grid has many aligned marks. It is tempting to infer a generic rule such as “paint every gap between collinear marks.” Even when some demos visibly add a second color in row/column gaps, that rule must not be transferred unless it reproduces the *entire changed-cell mask and unchanged cells in every demo* under the same role-level interpretation.

## Evidence ledger
For each pair, record:
- background and mark/output colors as roles, not fixed values;
- exact coordinates of all input marks;
- every changed output coordinate, grouped by row/column and local motif;
- mark cells and background cells that remain untouched;
- whether a candidate line/segment rule predicts all changed and unchanged locations.

The examples can exhibit added color-2 cells along some aligned gaps, but the test's dense cross-axis alignments make blanket row/column interpolation a high-impact assumption. Repeatedly resubmitting that same gap-fill hypothesis with minor variants is not new evidence and wastes attempts. Do not claim a model is validated merely because it resembles one demonstration.

## Recovery after rejection
After a failed submission, preserve the rejection as evidence against the exact candidate. Identify which competing rule would change the test output, then check whether the existing demonstrations discriminate them. If the demonstrations do not resolve it and demo budget remains, request a discriminating pair; once exhausted, avoid cosmetic replays and submit only a materially different hypothesis supported by the complete set. Prefer a conservative, mechanically checked candidate over copying a previous failed grid.

## Validation gate
Before submit, replay the candidate over every demo pair. It must match output dimensions, all additions/removals/recolors, and all identity/background positions. For line completion specifically, distinguish among: filling only one-cell holes, filling gaps between consecutive markers, filling the entire extrema span, extending beyond endpoints, and triggering only where crossing lines/motifs exist. Never conflate these variants.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-rnd0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u043/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/heterogeneous-line-fill-overreach.md"}
